<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  UTF-8 process code page application manifest, embedded in the three apps.

  The element and its namespace are the ones Microsoft documents for `activeCodePage`
  (win32/sbscs/application-manifests): `UTF-8` is the only value valid on Windows 10 and is
  case-sensitive, and the element was first added in Windows 10 version 1903 (May 2019 Update).
  Declaring it and running on an earlier build is allowed, and there the process keeps its legacy
  code page, so the element is an opt-in the system honours where it exists rather than a guarantee
  everywhere.

  It is a default because it repairs an observable defect and has no performance arm to compare
  against, but it is measured rather than asserted (2026-10-09, a probe built twice from one source,
  one `/MANIFESTINPUT` apart, in this toolchain): `GetACP` and `GetOEMCP` read 65001 with it against
  1252 and 437 without, and a narrow `fopen` of a UTF-8 name created the correct name with it against
  a cp1252-mis-decoded one without (the on-disk names were the codepoints U+4E2D U+6587 against
  U+00E4 U+00B8 U+00AD U+00E6 U+2013 U+2021). No code of these apps sets a console code page
  (`WriteConsoleW`, `wprintf`, `wmain` and `_setmode` all have zero occurrences under apps/), and the
  manifest does NOT change the console: both probe builds report `GetConsoleCP` and `GetConsoleOutputCP`
  437, so a console whose code page is not UTF-8 still renders this process's bytes its own way. The
  narrow APIs, `argv` and file names are what this fixes; the console itself needs
  `SetConsoleOutputCP(CP_UTF8)`, which the three apps now call (2026-10-10). Measured with a probe that
  reads both code pages inside the console the app ran in: output CP 437 before, 65001 after, input CP
  437 in both, and 437 with the pre-fix binary in the same sequence. The call sets the output side only,
  which is the side rendering uses. Note that `chcp` reports the *input* code page and therefore cannot
  show this: a `chcp` based check reads 437 with and without the fix, which is how the first attempt at
  verifying it failed. diffutils ships the same
  file under the same name (`windows-utf8.manifest`), which is the same pattern reached independently.

  Namespace strings are silent-fail: an unrecognised `windowsSettings` namespace is ignored rather
  than reported, so the URL below is the documented one for this element and is deliberately not the
  2020 namespace used by `cmake/segment_heap.manifest` for `heapType`. The file itself is not
  silent-fail: the linker parses it, so a malformed XML comment (a double-hyphen sequence in prose is
  invalid) fails the build with mt.exe c1010070 rather than being dropped. That is not hypothetical:
  this comment carried one twice, once while it was written and once while the sentence describing it
  was added, and both times the link stopped until it was removed.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0"
          xmlns:asmv3="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
  <asmv3:application>
    <asmv3:windowsSettings xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">
      <activeCodePage>UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </asmv3:windowsSettings>
  </asmv3:application>
</assembly>
